## 2a. Explain the code: what it does, what is printed/not, and why

### The code below creates a matrix of 3 rows x 3 columns dimension. The values are integers from 1-9 and are arranged by column, because R defaults to filling in each row per column order to fill matrices. Since there is no specific command to print after the creation of the matrix, a successful run of this code will not print anything.

```{r 2a-1, eval=TRUE, echo=TRUE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, class.source = "fold-show", fig.height=5, fig.width=8, results='show', cache=FALSE}

mat <- matrix(1:9, nrow = 3, ncol = 3)
```

### The code below will look for the index of the maximum number in the matrix. Because of the order of the matrix, the maximum number is 9 and it is found in the ninth index, therefore R prints the index.

```{r 2a-2, eval=TRUE, echo=TRUE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, class.source = "fold-show", fig.height=5, fig.width=8, results='show', cache=FALSE}

which.max(mat)
```

### The purpose of the code: which.max(as.data.frame(mat)), is to look for the index of the maximum number/value in a data frame. However, when run the result prints an error message: *Error in which.max(as.data.frame(mat)): 'list' object cannot be coerced to type 'double'*, because the matrix is not a data frame. The function which.max() only works with vectors, so it won't work for a data frame.

### The code: mat[1:9,] , is asking to subset the matrix from rows 1-9 and all of the columns. When run, the result prints an error message: *Error in mat[1:9, ]: subscript out of bounds*. This is because the matrix only has 3 rows and it cannot call upon non-existent rows.

### The code: mat[,1:9], is asking to subset the matrix from columns 1-9 and all of the rows. When run, the result is an error message: *Error in mat[,1:9]: subscript out of bounds*. This is because the matrix only has 3 columns and similarly with the code above it cannot call upon non-existent columns.


## 2b. Explain the code: what it does, what is printed/not, and why

### The code below creates a matrix of 3 rows x 3 columns dimension. The values are integers from 1-5, two sevens, and two NA (which is missing value). The argument *byrow=T* forces the matrix to be filled per row instead of the R default of filling in per column order. The result prints the matrix and its values in row x column dimension, because the code specifies a print() function.

```{r, eval=TRUE, echo=TRUE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, class.source = "fold-show", fig.height=5, fig.width=8, results='show', cache=FALSE}

