## Question 3.g. Create a volcano plot of the differential expression analysis that was performed on the downloaded data and describe how to interpret this plot. Use ggplot2!

A volcano plot is a visualization tool used in gene expression analysis to identify genes that have undergone significant changes in expression between two experimental conditions. By plotting the log2 fold change on the x-axis and the negative log10 p-value on the y-axis, volcano plots allow quick assessment of both the magnitude and statistical significance of gene expression differences.

Genes located in the upper right quadrant of the plot represent genes that have been significantly up-regulated (increased expression) with high statistical significance. Conversely, genes in the upper left quadrant indicate significant down-regulation (decreased expression). Genes near the center of the plot have minimal changes in expression.

The addition of threshold lines to the volcano plot can further aid in the interpretation of results. Horizontal lines can be used to define a p-value cutoff, while vertical lines can be used to specify a fold change threshold. Genes that exceed both thresholds are considered to be the most biologically relevant.

```{r, echo=FALSE}
#### Load the necessary library ####
library(ggplot2)

#### Create the volcano plot ####
ggplot(RNAseq_GSE220979_3a, aes(x = log2FoldChange, y = -log10(pvalue))) +
  geom_point(aes(color = pvalue < 0.05 & abs(log2FoldChange) > 0.5), 
             alpha = 0.6, size = 2) +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("grey", "magenta"),
                     labels = c("Below threshold", "Up-/Downregulated"),
                     name = "Significance") +  
  theme_minimal() +
  labs(title = "Volcano Plot", 
       x = "Log2 Fold Change", 
       y = "-Log10 P-value") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = c(-0.5, 0.5), linetype = "dashed", color = "blue") +  # Fold change threshold
  geom_hline(yintercept = -log10(0.05), linetype = "dashed", color = "blue")  # P-value threshold

```

## Question 3.h. Create a MA plot of the data using the ggplot2 plotting package. Describe how to interpret this plot.

An MA plot is a graphical tool used to visualize and interpret gene expression data. By plotting the average expression level (A) on the x-axis and the log fold change (M) on the y-axis, MA plots provide a clear representation of the relationship between gene expression and differential expression between two conditions.

Genes located far from the horizontal line at M = 0 indicate significant changes in expression, with points above the line representing up-regulation and points below the line representing down-regulation. The distance from the line corresponds to the magnitude of the fold change, with larger distances indicating more significant changes.

The distribution of points on an MA plot can reveal trends in the data set. For example, if there are more points above the M = 0 line, it may suggest a general trend toward up-regulation in the treatment condition. Additionally, clusters of points in certain areas can indicate groups of co-regulated genes or shared biological pathways.

MA plots can also help identify potential biases in the data. For example, if the points show a systematic shift in M values as a function of A (e.g., more down-regulated genes at low expression levels), it may indicate that lowly expressed genes are not reliably measured. This type of bias, known as a "low-expression bias," can affect the interpretation of differential expression results.

```{r, echo=FALSE}
#### Create the MA plot ####
ggplot(RNAseq_GSE220979_3a, aes(x = avg_expression, y = log2FoldChange)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.6) + 
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", color = "blue") +  # M = 0 line
  labs(title = "MA Plot", 
       x = "Average Expression (A)", 
       y = "Log2 Fold Change (M)") +
  theme_minimal()

```

