print("Count of categorical values")
print(categ_counts)
```

## In the case of categorical values, it's helpful to use a bar graph to dinstinguish the total count for each category because it's an integer value and not a statistical result. The code below will visualize the categorical count as a bar graph instead of a box plot.

```{r categorical_stats, echo=FALSE}
#### Load the necessary libraries ####
library(ggplot2)

#### Function to create bar graph for categories ####
bar_graph <- function(df, col_name) {
  categ_counts <- table(df[[col_name]], useNA="ifany")
  
  ####convert table to data frame for ggplot####
  categ_df <- as.data.frame(categ_counts)
  colnames(categ_df) <- c("Category", "Count")
  
  ####make the bar plot####
  ggplot(categ_df, aes(x=Category, y=Count)) +
    geom_bar(stat = "identity", fill="skyblue") +
    theme_minimal() +
    labs(title = paste("Bar graph of", col_name), x="Class Category", y="Total Count") +
    theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle=45, hjust=1))
}

####Execute the function####
bar_graph(iris_data, "class")
```
